    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="13" relname="span">Intuitiveness can also be written into a program.</segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="15" relname="span">There exists today an artificial intuition that is built into many applications.</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="21" relname="span">The programs themselves are using intuition.</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="3" relname="concession">Granted, what they are doing is looking for patterns in information.</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="16" relname="span">Websites like amazon.com are using intuitive data mining.</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="5" relname="elaboration">Based on what products a customer views or purchases the Amazon software will create an intuitive match of what other products might interest the buyer.</segment>
        <segment id="7" parent="18" relname="concession">It is artificial intelligence,</segment>
        <segment id="8" parent="18" relname="span">but making the argument that it is much different from a persons intuition would be difficult.</segment>
        <segment id="9" parent="19" relname="sequence">If two friends were in a bookstore</segment>
        <segment id="10" parent="19" relname="sequence">and one mentioned to the other that they like Steven King books,</segment>
        <segment id="11" parent="19" relname="sequence">the second person might suggest another horror author that they might enjoy.</segment>
        <segment id="12" parent="17" relname="summary">This is one area where the line between computer intuition and human gets blurry.</segment>
        <group id="13" type="span"/>
